+/**
+ * @file
+ *
+ * Point-to-point entry points for internal collective traffic.
+ *
+ * Every point-to-point message exchanged on behalf of a collective
+ * operation (the coll framework, communicator construction, dynamic
+ * process management, topology creation and collective I/O) goes
+ * through these functions instead of calling the PML directly.  User
+ * level MPI_Send/MPI_Recv and friends do not use them.
+ *
+ * The same holds for the request completion calls (wait/test/cancel/
+ * free) made on requests that belong to such internal traffic: this
+ * includes requests returned by the functions above as well as requests
+ * of internal sub-operations (e.g. a nonblocking collective started by
+ * a hierarchical collective or during communicator construction).
+ *
+ * The MPI request bindings (MPI_Wait, MPI_Waitall, MPI_Waitany,
+ * MPI_Waitsome, MPI_Test, MPI_Testall, MPI_Testany, MPI_Testsome,
+ * MPI_Cancel and MPI_Request_free) use them as well when the user
+ * completes a collective or communicator request, e.g. one returned by
+ * MPI_Ibcast, MPI_Bcast_init or MPI_Comm_idup; see
+ * ompi_coll_p2p_request_is_internal().  For a user point-to-point
+ * request they call the request engine directly, as before.
+ *
+ * The symbols follow the MPI_ / PMPI_ profiling scheme.  Each
+ * ompi_coll_p2p_<op>() is exported from libmpi as a weak alias of the
+ * strong Pompi_coll_p2p_<op>(), which holds the default implementation
+ * (a plain forward to the selected PML or request engine, with identical
+ * semantics and return codes).  A tool or an alternative transport
+ * interposes by defining its own ompi_coll_p2p_<op>() (in the executable,
+ * in a library linked ahead of libmpi, or via LD_PRELOAD) and reaches the
+ * default implementation by calling Pompi_coll_p2p_<op>().
+ */
